---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CourseStrip from '../../../../components/CourseStrip.astro';
import data from '../../../../data/topics.json';
import kidData from '../../../../data/kid-summaries.json';

const B = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') || '';
const { subjects, topics } = data;
const topic = topics.find((t) => t.slug === 'subjects/computer-science/foundational/growth-of-functions');
const kidSummary = kidData[topic.slug] ?? null;
const subject = subjects.find((s) => s.id === topic.subject);
const seq = topics.filter((t) => t.status !== 'planned');
const idx = seq.indexOf(topic);
const prev = seq[(idx - 1 + seq.length) % seq.length];
const next = seq[(idx + 1) % seq.length];

const growthCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'x', label: 'O(n)', color: '#0e7490' },
    { expr: 'x*log2(x)', label: 'O(n log n)', color: '#1f6f54' },
    { expr: 'x^2', label: 'O(n²)', color: '#b45309' },
    { expr: '2^(x/2)', label: 'O(2ⁿ)', color: '#b42318' },
  ],
  x: { min: 1, max: 22, label: 'input size n' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 460, label: 'operations' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 370,
};
---
<Layout title={`${topic.title} — ${subject.name} — Interactive Atlas`} description={topic.summary}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topic-grid">
      <aside class="topic-aside">
        <a class="back" href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>← all of {subject.name}</a>
        <dl>
          <dt>subject</dt><dd><span class="dot" style={`--dot:${subject.ink}; display:inline-block; margin-right:.4rem`}></span>{subject.name}</dd>
          <dt>level</dt><dd>{topic.level}</dd>
          <dt>time</dt><dd>{topic.minutes} min</dd>
          <dt>tags</dt><dd>{topic.tags.slice(0, 3).join(', ')}</dd>
        </dl>
      </aside>
      <article>
        <header class="topic-head">
          <p class="crumb"><a href={B + '/'}>atlas</a><span class="sep">/</span><a href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>{subject.name}</a><span class="sep">/</span>{topic.level}</p>
          <h1>{topic.title}</h1>
          <p class="lede">{topic.summary}</p>
        </header>
        <CourseStrip slug="subjects/computer-science/foundational/growth-of-functions" subject="computer-science" />

        {kidSummary && (
          <aside class="kid-note" aria-label="Summary for young readers">
            <p><strong>For young explorers:</strong> {kidSummary}</p>
          </aside>
        )}
        <div class="topic-body">
          <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
          <p>
            This is an early lesson in Computer Science. You need no programming experience —
            only arithmetic (including powers such as 2¹⁰) and the willingness to count
            carefully. Everything else is built here, step by step. The lesson sits at the
            gateway of the syllabus's algorithm-design unit: before you can design, test and
            amend algorithms, you need to understand what makes one algorithm <em>better</em>
            than another.
          </p>
          <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>explain what Big-O notation describes — the <em>shape</em> of a cost curve, not a time in seconds;</li>
            <li>read the common complexity classes — O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), O(n²), O(2ⁿ) — and name a real algorithm for each;</li>
            <li>design a solution the standard way: abstraction, decomposition, pseudocode before code;</li>
            <li>recognise the standard methods of solution — totalling, counting, max/min, linear and binary search, sorting, validation;</li>
            <li>build a test table with normal, boundary and erroneous data, and debug with a trace table;</li>
            <li>amend an algorithm safely, re-checking the shape of its cost after every change.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            One habit: whenever an algorithm appears, we ask how its cost <em>grows</em> before
            we ask how fast it runs. The growth shape decides everything at scale.
          </p>

          <h2>An algorithm's cost is a curve, not a number</h2>
          <p>
            Ask "how long does this program take?" and the honest answer is never "one
            second"; it is "one second <em>for this input — and what happens as the input
            grows?</em>" Two programs can both be correct, both run fine on your laptop, and
            yet one finishes in a blink and the other outlives the universe on a dataset a
            million times larger. Big-O notation names the difference: it describes the
            <strong>shape</strong> of the cost curve as input size <code>n</code> grows,
            ignoring constant factors and small-input noise, because shape is what decides
            everything at scale. Which programs finish and which spin forever, which datasets
            fit and which don't — that is decided by the curve, and the curves are
            surprisingly few. Four shapes dominate; here they are against input size
            <code>n</code>:
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 1</span><span>operations vs input size — the quadratic and exponential leave everything behind</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="plotter" data-config={JSON.stringify(growthCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>
          <p>
            <strong>What to notice:</strong> on the left edge the four curves are tangled
            together — at small inputs you could not order them by eye. Slide right and the
            ordering asserts itself, then the gaps explode: the teal linear curve is flat as
            a floor next to the red exponential. That left-edge tangle is not a defect of
            the plot; it is the single most practical fact in this subject, and we return to
            it below.
          </p>

          <h2>Reading the curve: the common complexity classes</h2>
          <p>
            Work through the shapes with real numbers. <strong>O(1)</strong>, constant: the
            cost does not depend on n at all — looking up an array element by index takes one
            step whether the array has 10 slots or 10 million. <strong>O(log n)</strong>,
            logarithmic: each step throws away half the remaining input. Binary search on a
            million sorted items needs about 20 steps (2²⁰ ≈ 10⁶); on a billion items, about
            30. Doubling the data adds exactly one step. <strong>O(n)</strong>, linear: touch
            every item once — summing a list, finding a maximum. Double the data, double the
            work.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>O(n log n)</strong> is merge sort's signature — log n levels of work,
            each level touching n items; this is the practical floor for comparison-based
            sorting, and it stays near-linear forever. <strong>O(n²)</strong>, quadratic:
            compare every item with every other — bubble sort, checking all pairs. n = 1,000
            costs a million steps (fine); n = 100,000 costs ten billion (not fine).
            <strong>O(2ⁿ)</strong>, exponential: try every subset — brute-forcing which items
            to pack in a knapsack. This is the compound-interest curve of the mathematical
            growth family: each extra unit of input <em>multiplies</em> the work by a fixed
            growth factor instead of adding to it, which is why every exponential eventually
            overtakes every polynomial, whatever the constants. At n = 60 there are more
            steps than grains of sand on Earth. Brute force dies here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>So far:</strong> a handful of shapes cover almost every algorithm you
            will meet. Next: what Big-O does <em>not</em> tell you, and how the shapes get
            designed into programs in the first place.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Common misconception:</em> Big-O is not a running time and not a prediction
            in seconds — it is a growth <em>rate</em>. "O(n²) takes longer than O(n)" is only
            guaranteed <em>asymptotically</em>: for tiny inputs the "worse" algorithm can
            win, because Big-O hides constant factors. Insertion sort (O(n²)) genuinely beats
            merge sort on lists of a dozen items — which is exactly why real libraries hand
            small sublists to insertion sort and only deploy the asymptotic winner at scale.
            Zoom into the left edge of the plot above and you can see the curves tangle;
            shape only asserts itself at scale, and that is why engineers profile before
            optimising.
          </p>
          <p>
            One refinement completes the picture: time is not the only cost with a shape.
            <strong>Space complexity</strong> — how much memory an algorithm needs as n grows
            — obeys the same notation, and the two trade against each other constantly: merge
            sort needs O(n) extra space to gain its O(n log n) speed, while heapsort sorts in
            place with O(1) extra memory. Caching effects mean "fewer bytes moved" is often
            the real reason one algorithm beats another, which is why complexity analysis and
            measurement are companions, not rivals.
          </p>

          <h2>Where curves come from: designing and creating a solution</h2>
          <p>
            Complexity is not an accident; it is designed in. The standard method for
            building an algorithm starts with <strong>abstraction</strong> — discarding
            details irrelevant to the problem (a route-planner keeps road connections and
            journey times, forgets scenery and tarmac) — followed by
            <strong>decomposition</strong>: break the problem into sub-problems until each is
            directly codeable. Design the algorithm <em>before</em> the code: in structured
            English, in pseudocode, or as a flowchart. Pseudocode is the working language
            because it forces every decision (what loop, what condition, what variables)
            while staying implementation-free.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two design habits matter more than cleverness. First, choosing the right
            <strong>data structure</strong>: a sorted array enables binary search — O(log n)
            where a linear scan would pay O(n); a set makes membership checks constant-time.
            Second, recognising nested work. The heuristic that catches most disasters early:
            <em>count the loops</em>. One loop over n items is O(n); a loop inside a loop
            over the same n is usually O(n²); a loop that halves the problem each pass is
            O(log n). The widget's curves are, to first approximation, just loop-nesting made
            visible.
          </p>

          <h2>Standard methods of solution</h2>
          <p>
            Most algorithms are assembled from a small toolkit of patterns, and examiners
            (and interviewers) expect you to recognise them by name. <strong>Totalling</strong>:
            initialise an accumulator to 0 before a loop, add each value inside it.
            <strong>Counting</strong>: the same skeleton, but add 1 only when a condition
            holds — count the passing grades, the negative temperatures, the vowels.
            <strong>Maximum/minimum</strong>: set the running best to the first value, then
            replace it whenever a new value beats it. <strong>Linear search</strong>: walk
            the list until you find the target or run out — O(n), works on any order.
            <strong>Binary search</strong>: check the middle item, discard the half that
            cannot contain the target, repeat — O(log n), but it demands sorted data.
            <strong>Sorting</strong>: bubble sort swaps adjacent out-of-order pairs
            repeatedly — O(n²) but simple; merge sort splits, sorts the halves recursively,
            and merges — O(n log n). <strong>Validation</strong> (check input is sensible
            before accepting: range checks, type checks, presence checks) and
            <strong>verification of totals</strong> (checksums) round out the standard
            patterns. Knowing these cold is what "standard methods of solution" means: you
            are not inventing algorithms, you are recognising which classic the problem
            secretly is.
          </p>
          <p>
            The binary search's sorted-data condition is worth pausing on, because it is the
            classic exam trap. Sorting costs n log n <em>once</em>; after that, every search
            is logarithmic — which is why databases fight so hard to stay indexed. But run
            binary search on an unsorted list and it confidently returns wrong answers: the
            algorithm's guarantee is only as good as its precondition. Before using any
            standard method, check what it assumes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>So far:</strong> algorithms are designed before they are coded, from a
            named toolkit of standard methods — each carrying preconditions you must check
            before use. A design, though, is only a hypothesis until it meets test data,
            and the trace table is where wrong steps become visible.
          </p>

          <h2>Test data, trace tables and debugging</h2>
          <p>
            A program that works on the happy path is not yet correct. <strong>Test
            data</strong> comes in planned categories. <em>Normal</em> data: values the
            program should accept and process correctly (a valid mark of 65).
            <em>Boundary</em> (extreme) data: the exact edges of valid ranges — for marks
            valid in 0–100, test 0, 100, and the neighbours −1 and 101; off-by-one errors
            (using <code>&lt;</code> where <code>&lt;=</code> belonged) are invisible to
            normal tests and fatal at the boundary. <em>Erroneous (abnormal)</em> data:
            values that should be rejected with a sensible message, not a crash or silent
            nonsense ("twenty", a mark of 999, a blank field). The discipline is to design
            the test table <em>before</em> running anything, stating each input and its
            expected output — otherwise you will unconsciously test what the program does
            well.
          </p>
          <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "it worked on my test data, so it works." Normal
          data is the least informative category, precisely because correct and incorrect
          programs agree on it. The boundary values are where correct and incorrect programs
          split: a range check that excludes 100 and one that includes it produce identical
          behaviour for every mark from 1 to 99 — and opposite verdicts for exactly one
          input. If your test table has no edges in it, it has not tested anything yet.</p>
          <p>
            When it does go wrong, the primary manual tool is the <strong>trace table</strong>:
            a grid with a column per variable, one row per pass through the loop, recording
            every change of value. Tracing swaps in a bubble sort on the list [5, 2, 9] —
            watching first-pass comparisons and swaps unfold row by row — exposes in seconds
            whether the algorithm ever touches what it should. Trace tables catch three
            failure families: loops that run once too often (the final row shows a value
            creeping past its bound), counters mis-initialised (a total that starts at 1
            instead of 0 poisons every later row), and conditions that never fire (a column
            that stays at its initial value for the whole run). Modern practice adds
            debuggers, print statements and automated test suites, but they all implement the
            same idea: slow the program down until the wrong <em>step</em>, not just the wrong
            answer, is visible.
          </p>

          <h2>Writing and amending algorithms</h2>
          <p>
            The final skill is production: translating a design into clean pseudocode and
            then <em>amending</em> it safely — because requirements change. The rules of good
            algorithmic writing are unfashionable but real: meaningful identifier names
            (<code>totalHours</code>, not <code>t</code>), one entry and one exit point per
            structure, no duplicated logic (extract it into a function), and initialisation
            immediately before use. Amendments then become local: changing a validation
            range, adding a counting branch, swapping a linear search for a binary one — each
            is a one-line edit in a well-named algorithm and a debugging nightmare in a
            tangled one. When you amend, re-run the trace table on the new logic and re-check
            the boundaries you did not touch; the classic amendment bug is fixing one
            boundary and breaking its twin. And after every change, re-ask the complexity
            question: did this edit change the <em>shape</em>? A "small fix" that nests one
            loop inside another has moved the program from the teal curve to the orange one,
            and no amount of hardware heroics will fully buy that back.
          </p>

          <h2>One task, three algorithms: a worked comparison</h2>
          <p>
            Concrete numbers make the curves land. Task: find a single name in a sorted list
            of 1,000,000 entries. <em>Linear search</em> (O(n)): on average half the list —
            500,000 comparisons; at 100 ns per comparison, 50 milliseconds — imperceptible
            once. <em>Binary search</em> (O(log n)): about 20 comparisons — 2 microseconds,
            25,000× faster.
          </p>
          <p>
            Now scale to a billion entries: linear becomes 50 seconds per lookup (per
            lookup!), binary is still ~30 comparisons. The <em>sort</em> that enables binary
            search is a one-time O(n log n) cost — about 20 billion comparisons for the
            billion-entry list, a few minutes once — after which every one of the millions of
            daily lookups is logarithmic. Amortise and the trade is a landslide. Now the
            other direction: a bubble sort on that same billion entries at n² = 10¹⁸
            operations is, at 100 ns each, roughly <em>three thousand years</em>. Same
            machine, same data, same problem — the only variable was the shape of the
            algorithm's cost curve. This is why "growth of functions" is not an abstract
            maths detour but the difference between a feature that ships and one that never
            finishes its first run.
          </p>
          <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "slow program? Buy a faster computer." A 1,000×
          faster chip buys a quadratic algorithm roughly 10√1000 ≈ 31.6 times the input —
          not 1,000 times — and an exponential one less than 10 extra items, while a linear
          algorithm gets the full 1,000. Hardware multiplies a fixed budget; a better
          algorithm changes the curve. This asymmetry is why complexity is called the one
          free lunch in computer science, and why "can we just throw hardware at it?" is a
          question with a shape-dependent answer.</p>

          <h2>Check yourself</h2>
          <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
          <ol>
            <li>An algorithm halves its remaining work every step. Which class is it, and what happens to its cost when the input doubles?</li>
            <li>A friend's bubble-sort program "feels fine" on their 50-item playlist. What would you test to see the difference from merge sort, and where does the difference actually appear?</li>
            <li>For a program that accepts marks from 0 to 100 inclusive, list the boundary test inputs and say what each one defends against.</li>
            <li>You replace a single loop with a loop inside a loop, both over n items. What changed, and why does "it still works" not settle the question?</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) O(log n) — logarithmic. Doubling the input adds
            exactly one step; a billion items need about 30. (2) Both are correct and both
            feel fine at 50 items — the difference is asymptotic, so you would compare growth:
            time both on 50, then 5,000, then 500,000 items and watch the quadratic pull away
            as n² while n log n stays near-linear. At n = 50 the "worse" algorithm may
            genuinely win. (3) 0 and 100 (the accepted edges — catches range checks using the
            wrong comparison), and −1 and 101 (the rejected neighbours — catches off-by-one
            errors that leak invalid values in, or spill valid ones out). (4) The class went
            from O(n) to O(n²) — the teal curve to the orange one. "It still works" speaks to
            correctness, which is orthogonal: the program is right and also unsustainable at
            scale, which is exactly the distinction this lesson exists to make visible.
          </p>

          <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Big-O names the shape of a cost curve as input grows — a growth rate, never a time in seconds.</li>
            <li>Six classes cover the everyday territory: O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), O(n²), O(2ⁿ) — halving is logarithmic, nesting is quadratic, multiplying-per-step is exponential.</li>
            <li>Shape dominates only at scale: constant factors hide in the notation, so small inputs can reverse the ranking — profile before optimising.</li>
            <li>Design before code: abstraction, decomposition, pseudocode — and count the loops to predict the class early.</li>
            <li>Standard methods are a named toolkit; each carries preconditions (binary search demands sorted data) that you must check.</li>
            <li>Test normal, boundary and erroneous data — edges are where correct and incorrect programs differ.</li>
            <li>Trace tables slow a program until the wrong step is visible; re-run them after every amendment, and re-ask whether the amendment changed the shape.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
          <p>
            The next lessons in this track put the toolkit to work: searching and sorting
            algorithms examined in full, with their trace tables and their costs; then graphs
            and dynamic programming, where clever design collapses exponential brute force
            into polynomial time; and finally computability and complexity, where the
            exponential wall itself becomes the object of study — which problems provably
            cannot be solved fast at all. The curve vocabulary from this lesson is the
            language every one of them is written in.
          </p>

          <h2>Going deeper</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Big-O is an upper bound</strong>, stated with its own algebra: constants
            drop (O(3n² + 500n) = O(n²)), products add in sequence (an O(n) pass followed by
            an O(n log n) sort is O(n log n)), and nested loops multiply.
            <strong>Ω (omega)</strong> and <strong>Θ (theta)</strong> refine the story —
            lower bound and tight bound — and the deepest fact in the field lives there:
            comparison-based sorting provably cannot beat n log n, so merge sort isn't just
            good, it is optimal-in-class. Harder still are the <strong>NP-hard</strong>
            problems — travelling salesman, knapsack at scale, timetabling — where every known
            algorithm is exponential and thousands of researchers suspect (the unresolved
            P vs NP question, the most famous open problem in computer science) that no fast
            algorithm exists at all. Practice there means heuristics: approximate answers,
            fast, rather than exact answers, never. The plot above is, in the end, a map of
            the computational universe: the flatlands where everything is easy, the hills
            where careful design is needed, and the walls beyond which computation itself
            runs out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Knuth, The Art of Computer Programming, Vol. 3 (sorting and searching); Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest & Stein, Introduction to Algorithms (4th ed.); Cambridge International AS & A Level Computer Science 9618 syllabus (algorithm design and problem-solving). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

        <nav class="topic-next" aria-label="Lesson navigation">
          <div><span class="tn-label">previous lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${prev.slug}/`}>{prev.title}</a></div>
          <div style="text-align:right"><span class="tn-label">next lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${next.slug}/`}>{next.title}</a></div>
        </nav>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
